From: PortareOS <hello@portare.org>
Subject: [PATCH] ASoC: qcom: derive the MI2S bit clock from the stream

The third and last thing pinning this link to 48 kHz, and the only one
that is about the hardware rather than about Linux bookkeeping.

The board's sound node names LPASS_CLK_ID_SEN_MI2S_IBIT as "i2s_clk"
and fixes it:

	assigned-clock-rates = <1536000>;

1536000 is 48000 x 2 x 16. The primary I2S patch enables that clock at
startup, so the link runs at a 48 kHz bit clock whatever the DAI masks
say, confirmed on hardware while playing:

  LPASS_CLK_ID_SEN_MI2S_IBIT  1  1  0  1536000 ... sound  i2s_clk

Set it from the stream instead. 44.1 kHz then gets 1411200 and the
device tree value becomes a default rather than a ceiling.

Done in the machine prepare op, which soc_pcm_prepare() calls before
snd_soc_dapm_stream_event(), so the clock is already at the right rate
when the aw88166 amps check their PLL against BCLK at DAPM PRE_PMU.
That ordering is what the primary I2S patch exists to protect and this
keeps it.

A q6dsp clock only tells the DSP its rate when it is prepared, and
startup has already prepared this one, so clk_set_rate() alone lands on
the next stream. The first version did only that, and every rate change
failed one stream late:

  48000 -> 44100   port starts on 1536000   APM_CMD_GRAPH_START refused
  44100 -> 48000   port starts on 1411200   APM_CMD_GRAPH_START refused

Asking for the same rate twice worked the second time, at 44100 as at
48000, with the amps quiet. So when the rate changes the clock is
unprepared, set and prepared again, which is balanced against the
enable in startup.

Only PRIMARY_MI2S_RX is touched, and a clk_set_rate that the clock
controller refuses is warned about rather than swallowed: if q6prm
cannot produce 1411200 this is where it says so.
--- a/sound/soc/qcom/sc8280xp.c
+++ b/sound/soc/qcom/sc8280xp.c
@@ -157,6 +157,46 @@
 	struct snd_soc_pcm_runtime *rtd = snd_soc_substream_to_rtd(substream);
 	struct snd_soc_dai *cpu_dai = snd_soc_rtd_to_cpu(rtd, 0);
 	struct sc8280xp_snd_data *data = snd_soc_card_get_drvdata(rtd->card);
+	struct snd_pcm_runtime *runtime = substream->runtime;
+
+	/*
+	 * The bit clock comes from a fixed assigned-clock-rates in the device
+	 * tree, 1536000, which is 48000 x 2 x 16. That makes 48 kHz the only
+	 * rate the link can carry however wide the DAI masks are. Derive it
+	 * from the stream instead, so 44.1 kHz content gets 1411200 and the
+	 * device tree value becomes a default rather than a ceiling.
+	 *
+	 * This runs before snd_soc_dapm_stream_event(), so the clock is at the
+	 * right rate before the aw88166 amps check their PLL against BCLK at
+	 * DAPM PRE_PMU, which is the ordering the primary I2S patch exists to
+	 * protect.
+	 */
+	if (cpu_dai->id == PRIMARY_MI2S_RX && data->i2s_clk && runtime) {
+		unsigned long bclk = (unsigned long)runtime->rate *
+				     runtime->channels *
+				     snd_pcm_format_width(runtime->format);
+		int ret;
+
+		/*
+		 * A q6dsp clock only reports its rate to the DSP when it is
+		 * prepared, and startup has already prepared this one. Setting
+		 * the rate alone would take effect on the next stream, so this
+		 * stream would start the port against the previous stream's
+		 * bit clock and the DSP refuses APM_CMD_GRAPH_START. Re-vote.
+		 */
+		if (clk_get_rate(data->i2s_clk) != bclk) {
+			clk_disable_unprepare(data->i2s_clk);
+			ret = clk_set_rate(data->i2s_clk, bclk);
+			if (ret)
+				dev_warn(rtd->card->dev,
+					 "could not set i2s bit clock to %lu: %d\n",
+					 bclk, ret);
+			ret = clk_prepare_enable(data->i2s_clk);
+			if (ret)
+				dev_err(rtd->card->dev,
+					"could not enable i2s bit clock: %d\n", ret);
+		}
+	}
 
 	return qcom_snd_sdw_prepare(substream, &data->stream_prepared[cpu_dai->id]);
 }
